GBA graphics: design for a gpanel backend
=========================================

Status: DONE.  Phase 1 (driver /dev/tft0), Phase 2 (image/glyph/font demos),
Phase 3 (Mode 5 double buffering /dev/tft1,tft2 + button input), Phase 4
(console/panel convergence) and Phase 4b (console backing-store redraw) are
implemented and real-HW verified, along with the A+B+START / A+B+R escape
chords.  This file is the original plan (sections 1-10, kept as the design
record) + implementation notes; a map, not a spec.


0. ESCAPE / FULL-SCREEN APP GUIDELINE  (read first if writing a demo)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

A+B+START held together is a "hardware Ctrl-C": swkbd_poll() sends SIGINT
to the console tty's foreground pgrp, so a program that owns /dev/tft0 (soft
keyboard hidden) can always be interrupted and the shell regained (its
close() -> gpanel_close() clears the screen).  (The combo avoids L/R/SELECT:
the GBA key matrix cannot distinguish certain 3-key combos - hardware key
ghosting - and L+R+SELECT is one of them; A+B+START reads cleanly.)

A+B+R is the same with SIGKILL, for a program that catches or ignores SIGINT.

Like a real Ctrl-C on this port, the signal is delivered on the target's next
syscall return, or - for user code that makes no syscalls - by preemption
from the Timer0 interrupt (gba_preempt_tramp, see NOTES.hardware), so even a
`for(;;);` spin can be interrupted.  A program that displays a static image
should still BLOCK (pause(), or a read) rather than spin, so it does not burn
the CPU other processes need; the bundled demos use `for(;;) pause();`.


1. GOAL
-------

Expose a pixel-addressable graphics device on the GBA so the existing
DiscoBSD graphics stack - the gpanel framework (/dev/tft0, sys/sys/gpanel.h,
lib/libgpanel, share/examples/gpanel) - runs unchanged on real GBA hardware
and in mGBA.  "Unchanged" is the whole point: we are NOT inventing a GBA
graphics API, we are adding a GBA BACKEND under the portable API that
pic32 already uses, so the example programs (pixel, line, rect, circle,
fill, color, font, and the games flappy / tftetris) and libgpanel work as-is.


2. WHAT ALREADY EXISTS (do not reinvent)
----------------------------------------

Portable layer (reuse verbatim where possible):
  - sys/sys/gpanel.h   - the ioctl ABI: GPANEL_CLEAR/PIXEL/LINE/RECT/FILL/
                         CIRCLE/IMAGE/CHAR/TEXT, the gpanel_font_t glyph
                         format, and the kernel-side struct gpanel_hw
                         backend (set_pixel / fill_rectangle / draw_image /
                         draw_glyph) + globals gpanel_width/gpanel_height.
  - lib/libgpanel      - userland wrappers (gpanel_open/clear/pixel/line/
                         rect/fill/circle/image/char/text/text_width/
                         fill_triangle).  ALREADY built for ARM (lib/Makefile
                         SUBDIR) as lib/libgpanel.a - what the cross-built GBA
                         userland links.  The on-device native toolchain has
                         no libgpanel (its libc is usr.bin/as/arm-dev/rt).
  - share/examples/gpanel - the "graphics commands": pixel/line/rect/fill/
                         circle/color/font/speed + games flappy, tftetris.
                         Fonts (5x7, 6x9, lucidasans*, verdana7, digits*)
                         are plain .c arrays in gpanel_font_t form.

PIC32 reference driver: sys/arch/pic32/dev/gpanel.c (705 lines).  It is
mostly machine-independent: the software rasterizers gpanel_draw_line,
gpanel_draw_circle, gpanel_draw_char/text and the GPANEL_* ioctl dispatch
all run on the abstract `hw` ops.  Only the lower part is PIC32-specific
(SPI/parallel bus access read_reg16/32, controller probe/attach, the
ili9341/st7781/... init_display backends, draw_logo).  GPANEL_MAJOR = 16
(sys/sys/conf.h).

GBA side before this work: video is already Mode 3 (240x160, 16bpp linear framebuffer
at VRAM = 0x06000000).  gba_text.c writes glyph pixels straight into it
(VRAM[y*240 + x] = colour, BGR555).  So the GBA is ALREADY the exact kind
of framebuffer gpanel wants - a gpanel pixel is one VRAM store.  conf.c
cdevsw slot 16 ("hxtft, gpanel, sgpanel") was reserved as NOCDEV; it now
holds gpanel_open/close/ioctl.


3. ARCHITECTURE
---------------

Backend ops needed by the portable raster (struct gpanel_hw):
    set_pixel(x,y,color)                 -> VRAM[y*240 + x] = conv(color)
    fill_rectangle(x0,y0,x1,y1,color)    -> clamped double loop of stores
    draw_image(x,y,w,h,data)             -> blit row-major 16-bit pixels
    draw_glyph(font,color,bg,x,y,w,bits) -> per-row bit unpack like gba_text
    resize(w,h)                          -> fixed 240x160, effectively no-op
gpanel_width = 240, gpanel_height = 160.

All of line/rect/circle/char/text/fill_triangle come "for free" from the
portable rasterizers once those four primitives exist.

Driver-structure decision (recommended): a SELF-CONTAINED GBA driver
    sys/arch/gba/dev/gba_gpanel.c
that contains (a) the GPANEL_* open/close/ioctl dispatch, (b) the software
rasterizers, and (c) the trivial VRAM backend.  The rasterizers are copied
from pic32 gpanel.c's MI portion (lines ~269-565) with the bus/probe/logo
code dropped.

Rationale over the alternatives:
  - vs. "compile pic32/dev/gpanel.c for GBA": that file drags in PIC32 bus
    access and controller probing; #ifdef'ing it up is uglier than a clean
    GBA file and couples two arches.
  - vs. "refactor gpanel.c into an MI core + per-arch backend": that is the
    tidy long-term shape, but it EDITS the working pic32 driver and its
    files/conf - risk to another port for no GBA benefit right now.  The
    standing rule is MI-safe + don't break what works.  So: ship the
    self-contained GBA driver first (zero pic32 changes); a possible later
    step is to hoist the shared rasterizers into sys/dev/gpanel_raster.c later and have
    BOTH arches include it.  The duplicated code is ~150 lines of stable,
    well-understood raster math.

Forward-compatibility requirement: expose the GBA framebuffer primitives
(set_pixel / fill_rectangle / blit-scroll / xor-cell / clear / draw_glyph)
as KERNEL-INTERNAL functions in gba_gpanel.c from day one, not buried in the
ioctl switch.  The char-device ioctls call them, and - crucially - the text
console and swkbd can LATER be migrated onto the same functions (see section
5.1).  Designing Phase 1 this way means the convergence is a rewiring of
callers, not a rewrite.


4. COLOUR FORMAT  (the one real gotcha)
---------------------------------------

The portable code and the example data assume 16-bit RGB565 (the pic32 TFT
panels' native order: R<<11 | G<<5 | B).  The GBA framebuffer is 15-bit
BGR555 (B<<10 | G<<5 | R, bit15 unused).  So the GBA backend's set_pixel
(and fill/image/glyph) must convert RGB565 -> BGR555:

    r = (c >> 11) & 0x1f;        /* 5 bits */
    g = (c >> 6)  & 0x1f;        /* top 5 of the 6 green bits */
    b =  c        & 0x1f;        /* 5 bits */
    bgr555 = (b << 10) | (g << 5) | r;

Done once in set_pixel so every primitive, image and glyph is correct, and
so the shipped fonts/examples (written for RGB565) show the right colours.
gba_text.c keeps its own BGR555 constants and is untouched.  (Image data in
share/examples is RGB565 too, so it converts consistently.)


5. CONSOLE / KEYBOARD COEXISTENCE  (must-fix, or graphics gets scribbled on)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

The text console and graphics share the ONE Mode 3 framebuffer.  Async
writers paint it from the kernel (machdep.c) and would corrupt a graphics
screen if left running:
  - the block cursor blink: gtxt_cursor(lbolt < hz/2)       (idle())
  - the on-screen keyboard:  swkbd_poll()                    (console_input_poll())
  - the idle screen-blank:   gtxt_blank(1)                   (idle())

Implemented as a single "graphics active" flag owned by the gpanel driver,
gba_gfx_active() in gba_gpanel.c:
  - gpanel_open()  sets it (and switches the video mode);
  - gpanel_close() clears it (console resumes; see redraw note below).
  - machdep.c idle() skips the cursor-blink and the screen-blank while it is
    set; swkbd stays hidden (do not draw the keyboard over graphics).
  - gba_text.c: console output and the keyboard stay silent while it is
    set (the text still goes to the backing store, see below).

Restoring the console on close: v1 had no text backing store (the console
was pixels only), so the next console output just scrolled from the cursor
row.  Phase 4b added a character backing store in spare VRAM (0x06015000,
gba_text.c), and gtxt_restore() repaints the console from it on close.


5.1  CONVERGENCE: one framebuffer owner (the clean fix for section 5)
---------------------------------------------------------------------

The interim flag in section 5 papers over the real issue: the GBA
framebuffer currently has THREE independent writers (console, swkbd, a
future /dev/tft0 app) with no owner.  The structural fix is to converge the
existing GBA graphics foundation onto the panel layer so there is exactly
ONE owner of VRAM, and arbitration lives in that one place instead of being
sprinkled across machdep.c / swkbd.c / gba_text.c.  (The end-state,
after the self-contained driver proved out.)

End-state: gba_gpanel.c owns VRAM and exports the kernel-internal
primitives from section 3.  Its clients are:
    - the text console (gba_text.c)  - a built-in client;
    - the on-screen keyboard (swkbd.c) - an overlay client;
    - a userland process via /dev/tft0 - an exclusive client.
The panel layer tracks the active owner; while a userland client holds the
panel, the console/swkbd draws are suppressed at this single boundary, and
on release the console redraws itself.  The section-5 scribble problem then
disappears: there is no async scribble because there is one arbiter.

Scope (why this is tractable, not a rewrite): gba_text.c's direct VRAM
pokes are already concentrated in four helpers -
    gtxt_shift / gtxt_shift_full  -> panel blit-scroll (bulk row copy)
    gtxt_draw_cell                -> panel draw_glyph (also swkbd's only path)
    gtxt_cursor                   -> panel xor-cell  (p[] ^= 0x7FFF invert)
    gtxt_cls                      -> panel clear
and swkbd.c already draws ONLY through gtxt_draw_cell.  So converging is:
route those helpers through the panel primitives (same pixels, just one
owner), move the owner flag/arbitration into the panel, and - the one real
addition - give the console a small CHARACTER backing store (<=40x20 cells
+ attribute, ~1-2 KB) so it can redraw after a graphics app or screen-blank
(this also cleans up the blank restore and would let vi repaint without a
full app redraw).  Note the two
ops the gpanel ABI lacks but the console needs - a scroll/blit and an
xor-cursor - stay as internal panel entry points, not gpanel ioctls.

Cost / risk: GBA-internal only (gba_text.c, swkbd.c, machdep.c idle hooks,
gba_gpanel.c) - still MI-safe, no other arch touched.  But it edits the
working console, so it is deliberately a LATER phase (see section 9), done
once the panel primitives are trusted; the section-5 flag ships first and is
removed when this lands.


6. VIDEO MODE
-------------

Mode 3 (single 240x160x16bpp buffer) is the default panel, /dev/tft0.  Pros:
full resolution, 16bpp, zero mode-switch, shares the console's VRAM.  Con: no
double buffering, so a full-screen redraw every frame can tear.  It is the
right choice for static demos and for INCREMENTAL animation (erase+redraw only
the changed region), which does not tear noticeably and is the cheapest path.

Mode 5 (IMPLEMENTED) adds double buffering: two 160x128x16bpp pages (0x06000000
and 0x0600A000, page-flipped via DISPCNT bit4), presented with the GBA-only
GPANEL_FLIP ioctl (sys/sys/gpanel.h, _IO('g',10)) which swaps pages at vblank.
gpanel has no page-flip concept, so FLIP is a GBA extension - a no-op on single-
buffered panels.  The 160x128 pages are smaller than the screen, so BG2's affine
transform places them; this is exposed as two devices:
  /dev/tft1  BG2 scales 160x128 up to fill the whole 240x160 screen.
  /dev/tft2  1:1, centred with a black border (pixel-exact 160x128).
The scaling is done by the PPU per-scanline, so it costs ~0 CPU - tft1 and tft2
run at the same speed; pick by whether full-screen or pixel-exact is wanted.

COST NOTE (real-HW, Phase 3).  The dominant cost of double-buffered
animation is NOT the mode or the scaling, it is how much of the back page you
repaint.  A naive "clear the whole 160x128 page + redraw" every frame writes
~20,500 px/frame and ran ~4x slower than Mode 3 (and on GBAED stuttered when
the big writes contended with SD/swap).  Tracking the previous sprite position
PER PAGE (two pages => two frames stale) and erasing only that rect drops it to
~hundreds of px/frame - as fast as Mode 3, no stutter.  So: use double buffering
for the tear-free present, but still draw incrementally.  (Mode 4's two 8bpp
paletted pages are a possible future variant; not needed so far.)


7. KERNEL INTEGRATION (mechanical)
----------------------------------

  - New file sys/arch/gba/dev/gba_gpanel.c; add to sys/arch/gba/conf/files.gba
    (standard, all three configs GBA/GBAED/GBALOG).
  - conf.c cdevsw slot 16: replace NOCDEV with gpanel_open/close/norw(read)/
    gpanel_write(or nowrite)/gpanel_ioctl/nullstop/0/seltrue/... and #include
    the gpanel header; guard with "#if GPANEL_MAJOR != 16 #error".  (read/
    write unused: gpanel is ioctl-driven, like the pic32 slot.)
  - Re-run config in each compile dir (GBA, GBAED, GBALOG) so files.gba is
    picked up - ioconf.c is generated but tracked in git, and its reconfig
    rule is mtime-based, so regenerate deliberately.
  - distrib/gba/mi.gba: add  cdev /dev/tft0  major 16  minor 0  mode 666.
    (libgpanel/examples default to "/dev/tft0".)


8. USERLAND
-----------

  - libgpanel: lib/libgpanel.a is already built for ARM (lib/Makefile) and
    is what the cross-built GBA example programs link (the on-device native
    toolchain has none, so the demos ship as cross-built binaries).  Nothing
    to port here; just link the demos against it in their GBA build.
  - Example programs: build a subset for the GBA image and/or ship sources
    for the on-device cc.  Start with the STATIC demos (no input):
    pixel, line, rect, circle, fill, color, font - these prove the pipeline.
    Then the interactive ones.
  - Input for interactive demos (flappy/tftetris): on pic32 they read the
    tty / stdin.  On GBA the "keyboard" is swkbd, which we hide during
    graphics.  So interactive demos need either (a) read the GBA buttons
    (REG_KEYINPUT) - cleanest for games - or (b) a minimal key path that
    still works with graphics active.  Done with (a): tetris, gball and
    gball5/gball5i read REG_KEYINPUT (share/examples/gba).

Test harness: the same libmgba route used for audio works for video too -
mgbarun (usr.bin/as/arm-dev) already sets a video buffer (core->setVideoBuffer); a small
tool can dump the 240x160 framebuffer to a PPM/PNG after N frames and diff
it against the expected shape (e.g. "rect drew a red box at x,y").  This
gives automated, ear-free verification before real-HW.


9. PHASING
----------

  Phase 1  Driver + node + one static demo.  gba_gpanel.c (kernel-internal
           primitives per section 3 + the GPANEL_* ioctls using them: pixel/
           fill/rect/line/circle + CLEAR size report), slot 16, /dev/tft0,
           the interim graphics-active flag gating idle() redraws (section 5).
           Verify with the framebuffer-dump harness: clear + rects/lines/
           circles.
  Phase 2  draw_image + draw_glyph/text + fonts; run font/color/image demos.
  Phase 3  Double buffering (Mode 5 page flip, GPANEL_FLIP) + button input.
           /dev/tft1 (scaled fullscreen) and /dev/tft2 (1:1 centred); demos
           read REG_KEYINPUT directly.  DONE + real-HW: see the section 6 cost
           note (draw incrementally, not whole-page, or it stutters on GBAED).
  Phase 4  CONVERGENCE (section 5.1): route gba_text.c + swkbd.c through the
           panel primitives and move owner arbitration into the panel.  This
           is the end-state; it retires the section-5 scribble problem
           structurally.  DONE + real-HW; gba_gfx_active() stayed as the
           single arbitration point that the console and swkbd test.
  Phase 4b Console character backing store (spare VRAM at 0x06015000) and
           its repaint on graphics close (gtxt_restore()).  DONE + real-HW.

  MI-safety: ALL phases touch ONLY sys/arch/gba/* and distrib/gba/mi.gba,
  with ONE additive shared edit: GPANEL_FLIP in sys/sys/gpanel.h (a new ioctl
  number, _IO('g',10)); no existing ABI changes, so pic32/stm32 are unaffected.
  No other shared sys/ edits.  The
  separate "hoist shared raster to sys/dev" dedup (section 3) is the only
  step that would touch other arches and is left for its own reviewed PR.


10. DECISIONS (open questions settled in Phase 1)
------------------------------------------------

  - Clip policy: clamp out-of-range pixels (safe) vs. trust the app.  pic32
    draw_char clamps; set_pixel does not.  GBA must clamp in set_pixel (a
    stray VRAM store past 240x160 hits other VRAM/OAM).  -> clamp.
  - Should gpanel_open() force the screen to black, or inherit whatever the
    console left?  -> inherit: gpanel_open() only switches the video mode;
    programs start with gpanel_clear() anyway.
  - Keep gba_text's Mode-3 assumption documented as a shared invariant so a
    future Mode-5 double-buffer change updates both.
